{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>On that day.</b> The meaning of <b>on that day</b> is that on that day the deliverance came, and not before; even though they had gone out of Egypt, they did not feel secure from them." "<b>And Israel saw… dead on the shore of the sea.</b> It is difficult: why was it necessary to inform us that he saw them dead? It is obvious that they would not be alive after drowning. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Mekhilta, Beshalah) said that they saw them in their dying, and the verse informs us thereby that there their death was completed. The reason was so that Israel would recognize them while they were still alive, and they would see Israel and shame would cover them. According to this, the verse's intention is in this manner: <b>And Israel saw the Egyptian</b> — whose soul was departing <b>on the shore of the sea</b>; and the word ״dead״ connects with the phrase ״on the shore of the sea״ and not with the word ״saw.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:11.594236569Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}